      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-23wf-ss-3",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "23WF(3) Informed consent to forensic procedures—general",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-23wf-ss-3"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-act-1914.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-act-1914.pdf, p.34",
      "values:originalText": "(3) If the suspect is in custody, the constable need not allow the\nsuspect to communicate, or attempt to communicate, with the legal\npractitioner in private if the constable suspects on reasonable\ngrounds that the suspect might attempt to destroy or contaminate\nany evidence that might be obtained by carrying out the forensic\nprocedure.",
      "skos:definition": "(3) If the suspect is in custody, the constable need not allow the\nsuspect to communicate, or attempt to communicate, with the legal\npractitioner in private if the constable suspects on reasonable\ngrounds that the suspect might attempt to destroy or contaminate\nany evidence that might be obtained by carrying out the forensic\nprocedure.",
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-23wf"
      },
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-23wf-ss-3-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-23wf-ss-3-logic-deontic-1"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "7a210431b9328e513c2a395ed25ae6234c558c89fc91f2f1532d59f736faa6fc",
      "skos:note": "Restricts communication with legal practitioner for suspects in custody"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-23wf-ss-3",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "23WF(3) Informed consent to forensic procedures—general",
      "values:kind": "subsection",
      "cof:pageNumber": 34,
      "values:originalText": "(3) If the suspect is in custody, the constable need not allow the\nsuspect to communicate, or attempt to communicate, with the legal\npractitioner in private if the constable suspects on reasonable\ngrounds that the suspect might attempt to destroy or contaminate\nany evidence that might be obtained by carrying out the forensic\nprocedure."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-23wf-ss-3-norm",
      "@type": "values:Prohibition",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-23wf-ss-3"
      },
      "values:deonticStatus": {
        "@id": "values:HeuristicDerived"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "values:borneBy": {
        "@id": "values:Agent"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-23wf-ss-3-logic-deontic-1",
      "@type": "cml:LogicApplication",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "cml:operator": "Prohibit",
      "cml:logicSummary": "Restricts action",
      "cml:executionSurface": "modalities::logic::deontic",
      "cml:confidence": {
        "@value": 1.0,
        "@type": "xsd:decimal"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:premiseText": "Constable has reasonable grounds to suspect evidence destruction",
      "cml:conclusionText": "Suspect cannot communicate with legal practitioner in private"
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-23wg",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "23WG Informed consent to forensic procedures—Aboriginal",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-23wg"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-act-1914.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-act-1914.pdf, p.34",
      "values:originalText": "persons and Torres Strait Islanders\n\n(1) This section applies where:\n\n(a) a constable intends to ask a suspect to consent to a forensic\nprocedure; and\n\n(b) the constable believes on reasonable grounds that the suspect\nis an Aboriginal person or a Torres Strait Islander.\n\n\nForensic procedures  Part ID\nForensic procedures on suspect by consent  Division 3\n\nSection 23WG\n\nCrimes Act 1914\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(2) A suspect not covered by section 23WE gives informed consent to\na forensic procedure if the suspect consents after a constable:\n\n(a) asks the suspect to consent to the forensic procedure under\nsection 23WH; and\n\n(b) informs the suspect, in accordance with the regulations and\nsection 23WJ, of the matters mentioned in that section; and\n\n(c) complies with the rest of this section.\n\n(3) The constable must not ask the suspect to consent to the forensic\nprocedure unless:\n\n(a) an interview friend is present; or\n\n(b) the suspect has expressly and voluntarily waived his or her\nright to have an interview friend present; or\n\n(c) the constable is a senior police officer and he or she believes\non reasonable grounds that, having regard to the suspect’s\nlevel of education and understanding, the suspect is not at a\ndisadvantage in relation to the request to consent by\ncomparison with members of the Australian community\ngenerally.\nNote:\nSection 23YK relates to proving a waiver under paragraph (3)(b) of\nthis section.\n\n(4) Before asking the suspect to consent to a forensic procedure, the\nconstable must:\n\n(a) inform the suspect that a representative of an Aboriginal\nlegal assistance organisation will be notified that the suspect\nis to be asked to consent to a forensic procedure; and\n\n(b) notify such a representative that the suspect is to be asked to\nconsent to a forensic procedure.\n\n(5) The constable is not required to comply with subsection (4) if:\n\n(a) he or she is aware that the suspect has arranged for a legal\npractitioner to be present while the suspect is asked to\nconsent to the forensic procedure; or\n\n(b) paragraph (3)(b) or (c) applies.",
      "skos:definition": "persons and Torres Strait Islanders\n\n(1) This section applies where:\n\n(a) a constable intends to ask a suspect to consent to a forensic\nprocedure; and\n\n(b) the constable believes on reasonable grounds that the suspect\nis an Aboriginal person or a Torres Strait Islander.\n\n\nForensic procedures  Part ID\nForensic procedures on suspect by consent  Division 3\n\nSection 23WG\n\nCrimes Act 1914\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(2) A suspect not covered by section 23WE gives informed consent to\na forensic procedure if the suspect consents after a constable:\n\n(a) asks the suspect to consent to the forensic procedure under\nsection 23WH; and\n\n(b) informs the suspect, in accordance with the regulations and\nsection 23WJ, of the matters mentioned in that section; and\n\n(c) complies with the rest of this section.\n\n(3) The constable must not ask the suspect to consent to the forensic\nprocedure unless:\n\n(a) an interview friend is present; or\n\n(b) the suspect has expressly and voluntarily waived his or her\nright to have an interview friend present; or\n\n(c) the constable is a senior police officer and he or she believes\non reasonable grounds that, having regard to the suspect’s\nlevel of education and understanding, the suspect is not at a\ndisadvantage in relation to the request to consent by\ncomparison with members of the Australian community\ngenerally.\nNote:\nSection 23YK relates to proving a waiver under paragraph (3)(b) of\nthis section.\n\n(4) Before asking the suspect to consent to a forensic procedure, the\nconstable must:\n\n(a) inform the suspect that a representative of an Aboriginal\nlegal assistance organisation will be notified that the suspect\nis to be asked to consent to a forensic procedure; and\n\n(b) notify such a representative that the suspect is to be asked to\nconsent to a forensic procedure.\n\n(5) The constable is not required to comply with subsection (4) if:\n\n(a) he or she is aware that the suspect has arranged for a legal\npractitioner to be present while the suspect is asked to\n",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-23wg-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-23wg-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-23wg-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-23wg-ss-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-23wg-ss-5"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "e085dafad30716bb4a53c07ec3b85caa7eb65f6a3a11e6975fa04849e4e30393"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-23wg",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "23WG Informed consent to forensic procedures—Aboriginal",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 34,
      "values:originalText": "persons and Torres Strait Islanders\n\n(1) This section applies where:\n\n(a) a constable intends to ask a suspect to consent to a forensic\nprocedure; and\n\n(b) the constable believes on reasonable grounds that the suspect\nis an Aboriginal person or a Torres Strait Islander.\n\n\nForensic procedures  Part ID\nForensic procedures on suspect by consent  Division 3\n\nSection 23WG\n\nCrimes Act 1914\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(2) A suspect not covered by section 23WE gives informed consent to\na forensic procedure if the suspect consents after a constable:\n\n(a) asks the suspect to consent to the forensic procedure under\nsection 23WH; and\n\n(b) informs the suspect, in accordance with the regulations and\nsection 23WJ, of the matters mentioned in that section; and\n\n(c) complies with the rest of this section.\n\n(3) The constable must not ask the suspect to consent to the forensic\nprocedure unless:\n\n(a) an interview friend is present; or\n\n(b) the suspect has expressly and voluntarily waived his or her\nright to have an interview friend present; or\n\n(c) the constable is a senior police officer and he or she believes\non reasonable grounds that, having regard to the suspect’s\nlevel of education and understanding, the suspect is not at a\ndisadvantage in relation to the request to consent by\ncomparison with members of the Australian community\ngenerally.\nNote:\nSection 23YK relates to proving a waiver under paragraph (3)(b) of\nthis section.\n\n(4) Before asking the suspect to consent to a forensic procedure, the\nconstable must:\n\n(a) inform the suspect that a representative of an Aboriginal\nlegal assistance organisation will be notified that the suspect\nis to be asked to consent to a forensic procedure; and\n\n(b) notify such a representative that the suspect is to be asked to\nconsent to a forensic procedure.\n\n(5) The constable is not required to comply with subsection (4) if:\n\n(a) he or she is aware that the suspect has arranged for a legal\npractitioner to be present while the suspect is asked to\nconsent to the forensic procedure; or\n\n(b) paragraph (3)(b) or (c) applies."
    },
