    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-23wn",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "23WN Circumstances in which senior police officer may order",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-23wn"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-act-1914.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-act-1914.pdf, p.45",
      "values:originalText": "non-intimate forensic procedures\n\nA senior police officer may order the carrying out of a\nnon-intimate forensic procedure on a suspect who is in custody if:\n\n(a) the suspect has been asked under Division 3 to consent to the\ncarrying out of the forensic procedure; and\n\n(b) the suspect has not consented; and\n\n(c) the senior police officer is satisfied as required by\nsection 23WO.",
      "skos:definition": "non-intimate forensic procedures\n\nA senior police officer may order the carrying out of a\nnon-intimate forensic procedure on a suspect who is in custody if:\n\n(a) the suspect has been asked under Division 3 to consent to the\ncarrying out of the forensic procedure; and\n\n(b) the suspect has not consented; and\n\n(c) the senior police officer is satisfied as required by\nsection 23WO.",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-23wn-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-23wn-logic-deontic-1"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "7ec11f118d819fdb2a0c06912e0fe8c5778622ec10b0ba9e9f822ce7fcad4beb",
      "skos:note": "Senior police officer may order non-intimate forensic procedures on suspect in custody under certain conditions."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-23wn",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "23WN Circumstances in which senior police officer may order",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 45,
      "values:originalText": "non-intimate forensic procedures\n\nA senior police officer may order the carrying out of a\nnon-intimate forensic procedure on a suspect who is in custody if:\n\n(a) the suspect has been asked under Division 3 to consent to the\ncarrying out of the forensic procedure; and\n\n(b) the suspect has not consented; and\n\n(c) the senior police officer is satisfied as required by\nsection 23WO."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-23wn-norm",
      "@type": "values:Obligation",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-23wn"
      },
      "values:deonticStatus": {
        "@id": "values:HeuristicDerived"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "values:borneBy": {
        "@id": "values:State"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": "borne by: State"
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-23wn-logic-deontic-1",
      "@type": "cml:LogicApplication",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "cml:operator": "may",
      "cml:logicSummary": "Implies obligation",
      "cml:executionSurface": "modalities::logic::deontic",
      "cml:confidence": {
        "@value": 1.0,
        "@type": "xsd:decimal"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-23wo",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "23WO Matters to be considered by senior police officer before",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-23wo"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-act-1914.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-act-1914.pdf, p.45",
      "values:originalText": "ordering forensic procedure\n\n(1) The senior police officer must be satisfied on the balance of\nprobabilities that:\n\n(a) the suspect is in the lawful custody of a constable; and\n\n(b) there are reasonable grounds to believe that the suspect\ncommitted a relevant offence; and\n\n(c) there are reasonable grounds to believe that the forensic\nprocedure is likely to produce evidence tending to confirm or\ndisprove that the suspect committed a relevant offence; and\n\n(d) the carrying out of the forensic procedure without consent is\njustified in all the circumstances.\n\n(2) In determining whether the carrying out of the forensic procedure\nwithout consent is justified in all the circumstances, the senior\npolice officer must balance the public interest in obtaining\nevidence tending to confirm or disprove that the suspect committed\nthe offence concerned against the public interest in upholding the\nphysical integrity of the suspect.\n\n(3) In balancing those interests, the senior police officer must have\nregard to the following matters:",
      "skos:definition": "ordering forensic procedure\n\n(1) The senior police officer must be satisfied on the balance of\nprobabilities that:\n\n(a) the suspect is in the lawful custody of a constable; and\n\n(b) there are reasonable grounds to believe that the suspect\ncommitted a relevant offence; and\n\n(c) there are reasonable grounds to believe that the forensic\nprocedure is likely to produce evidence tending to confirm or\ndisprove that the suspect committed a relevant offence; and\n\n(d) the carrying out of the forensic procedure without consent is\njustified in all the circumstances.\n\n(2) In determining whether the carrying out of the forensic procedure\nwithout consent is justified in all the circumstances, the senior\npolice officer must balance the public interest in obtaining\nevidence tending to confirm or disprove that the suspect committed\nthe offence concerned against the public interest in upholding the\nphysical integrity of the suspect.\n\n(3) In balancing those interests, the senior police officer must have\nregard to the following matters:",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-23wo-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-23wo-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-23wo-ss-3"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "bd4bcf075295243a479b965ab3e5af6afff60e085b5288dd508c6326e0585a62"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-23wo",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "23WO Matters to be considered by senior police officer before",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 45,
      "values:originalText": "ordering forensic procedure\n\n(1) The senior police officer must be satisfied on the balance of\nprobabilities that:\n\n(a) the suspect is in the lawful custody of a constable; and\n\n(b) there are reasonable grounds to believe that the suspect\ncommitted a relevant offence; and\n\n(c) there are reasonable grounds to believe that the forensic\nprocedure is likely to produce evidence tending to confirm or\ndisprove that the suspect committed a relevant offence; and\n\n(d) the carrying out of the forensic procedure without consent is\njustified in all the circumstances.\n\n(2) In determining whether the carrying out of the forensic procedure\nwithout consent is justified in all the circumstances, the senior\npolice officer must balance the public interest in obtaining\nevidence tending to confirm or disprove that the suspect committed\nthe offence concerned against the public interest in upholding the\nphysical integrity of the suspect.\n\n(3) In balancing those interests, the senior police officer must have\nregard to the following matters:"
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-23wo-ss-1",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "23WO(1) Matters to be considered by senior police officer before",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-23wo-ss-1"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-act-1914.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-act-1914.pdf, p.45",
      "values:originalText": "(1) The senior police officer must be satisfied on the balance of\nprobabilities that:\n\n(a) the suspect is in the lawful custody of a constable; and\n\n(b) there are reasonable grounds to believe that the suspect\ncommitted a relevant offence; and\n\n(c) there are reasonable grounds to believe that the forensic\nprocedure is likely to produce evidence tending to confirm or\ndisprove that the suspect committed a relevant offence; and\n\n(d) the carrying out of the forensic procedure without consent is\njustified in all the circumstances.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) The senior police officer must be satisfied on the balance of\nprobabilities that:\n\n(a) the suspect is in the lawful custody of a constable; and\n\n(b) there are reasonable grounds to believe that the suspect\ncommitted a relevant offence; and\n\n(c) there are reasonable grounds to believe that the forensic\nprocedure is likely to produce evidence tending to confirm or\ndisprove that the suspect committed a relevant offence; and\n\n(d) the carrying out of the forensic procedure without consent is\njustified in all the circumstances.",
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-23wo"
      },
