    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-23xp",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "23XP Medical practitioner or dentist of suspect’s choice may be",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-23xp"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-act-1914.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-act-1914.pdf, p.70",
      "values:originalText": "present for intimate forensic procedures\n\n(1) A suspect is entitled to request a medical practitioner or dentist (the\nexpert) of his or her choice as shown in the table in section 23XM\nto be present while a forensic procedure (other than a non-intimate\nforensic procedure) is carried out.\nNote:\nSection 23YE provides that the request may be made by the suspect’s\nlegal representative or interview friend.\n\n(2) The expert chosen is to be present at the forensic procedure unless\nhe or she:\n\n(a) is unable, or does not wish, to attend; or\n\n(b) cannot be contacted;\nwithin a reasonable time or, if relevant, within the time in which\nthe person responsible for the effective carrying out of the forensic\nprocedure considers the forensic procedure should be carried out if\nit is to be effective in affording evidence of the relevant offence.",
      "skos:definition": "present for intimate forensic procedures\n\n(1) A suspect is entitled to request a medical practitioner or dentist (the\nexpert) of his or her choice as shown in the table in section 23XM\nto be present while a forensic procedure (other than a non-intimate\nforensic procedure) is carried out.\nNote:\nSection 23YE provides that the request may be made by the suspect’s\nlegal representative or interview friend.\n\n(2) The expert chosen is to be present at the forensic procedure unless\nhe or she:\n\n(a) is unable, or does not wish, to attend; or\n\n(b) cannot be contacted;\nwithin a reasonable time or, if relevant, within the time in which\nthe person responsible for the effective carrying out of the forensic\nprocedure considers the forensic procedure should be carried out if\nit is to be effective in affording evidence of the relevant offence.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-23xp-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-23xp-ss-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "80b9b13bb78c49538a2cae554a9676726d334b49d10d188f79806d4c1d5b2318"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-23xp",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "23XP Medical practitioner or dentist of suspect’s choice may be",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 70,
      "values:originalText": "present for intimate forensic procedures\n\n(1) A suspect is entitled to request a medical practitioner or dentist (the\nexpert) of his or her choice as shown in the table in section 23XM\nto be present while a forensic procedure (other than a non-intimate\nforensic procedure) is carried out.\nNote:\nSection 23YE provides that the request may be made by the suspect’s\nlegal representative or interview friend.\n\n(2) The expert chosen is to be present at the forensic procedure unless\nhe or she:\n\n(a) is unable, or does not wish, to attend; or\n\n(b) cannot be contacted;\nwithin a reasonable time or, if relevant, within the time in which\nthe person responsible for the effective carrying out of the forensic\nprocedure considers the forensic procedure should be carried out if\nit is to be effective in affording evidence of the relevant offence."
    },
