      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:premiseText": "judicial proceeding means (other than in section 3L): a proceeding in or before a court; or a proceeding: before a body, or a person, acting under a law of the Commonwealth, of a State or of a Territory; and in which evidence may be taken on oath.",
      "cml:conclusionText": "judicial tribunal means a body or person (other than a court) before whom a judicial proceeding is conducted."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-34",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "34 Judge or magistrate acting oppressively or when interested",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-34"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-act-1914.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-act-1914.pdf, p.153",
      "values:originalText": "Excessive and unreasonable bail\n\n(1) A person commits an offence if:\n\n(a) the person is a judge or magistrate; and\n\n(b) the judge or magistrate is required or authorised by law to\nadmit a person accused of an offence to bail; and\n\n(c) the judge or magistrate requires excessive and unreasonable\nbail; and\n\n(d) the requirement is an abuse of the judge’s or magistrate’s\noffice; and\n\n(e) the offence referred to in paragraph (b) is an offence against a\nlaw of the Commonwealth.\nPenalty: Imprisonment for 2 years.\n\n(2) Subsection (1) does not apply if the judge or magistrate has a\nreasonable excuse.\nNote:\nA defendant bears an evidential burden in relation to the matter in\nsubsection (2) (see subsection 13.3(3) of the Criminal Code).\n\n(3) Absolute liability applies to the paragraph (1)(e) element of the\noffence.\nNote:\nFor absolute liability, see section 6.2 of the Criminal Code.\nActing when interested\n\n(4) A person commits an offence if:\n\n(a) the person is a judge or magistrate; and\n\n(b) the judge or magistrate perversely exercises jurisdiction in a\nmatter; and\n\n(c) the judge or magistrate has a personal interest in the matter;\nand",
      "skos:definition": "Excessive and unreasonable bail\n\n(1) A person commits an offence if:\n\n(a) the person is a judge or magistrate; and\n\n(b) the judge or magistrate is required or authorised by law to\nadmit a person accused of an offence to bail; and\n\n(c) the judge or magistrate requires excessive and unreasonable\nbail; and\n\n(d) the requirement is an abuse of the judge’s or magistrate’s\noffice; and\n\n(e) the offence referred to in paragraph (b) is an offence against a\nlaw of the Commonwealth.\nPenalty: Imprisonment for 2 years.\n\n(2) Subsection (1) does not apply if the judge or magistrate has a\nreasonable excuse.\nNote:\nA defendant bears an evidential burden in relation to the matter in\nsubsection (2) (see subsection 13.3(3) of the Criminal Code).\n\n(3) Absolute liability applies to the paragraph (1)(e) element of the\noffence.\nNote:\nFor absolute liability, see section 6.2 of the Criminal Code.\nActing when interested\n\n(4) A person commits an offence if:\n\n(a) the person is a judge or magistrate; and\n\n(b) the judge or magistrate perversely exercises jurisdiction in a\nmatter; and\n\n(c) the judge or magistrate has a personal interest in the matter;\nand",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-34-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-34-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-34-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-34-ss-4"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "f0628f8e7a89f74ba8b122220552de8147d539081ed033e91f115824339b4f06"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-34",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "34 Judge or magistrate acting oppressively or when interested",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 153,
      "values:originalText": "Excessive and unreasonable bail\n\n(1) A person commits an offence if:\n\n(a) the person is a judge or magistrate; and\n\n(b) the judge or magistrate is required or authorised by law to\nadmit a person accused of an offence to bail; and\n\n(c) the judge or magistrate requires excessive and unreasonable\nbail; and\n\n(d) the requirement is an abuse of the judge’s or magistrate’s\noffice; and\n\n(e) the offence referred to in paragraph (b) is an offence against a\nlaw of the Commonwealth.\nPenalty: Imprisonment for 2 years.\n\n(2) Subsection (1) does not apply if the judge or magistrate has a\nreasonable excuse.\nNote:\nA defendant bears an evidential burden in relation to the matter in\nsubsection (2) (see subsection 13.3(3) of the Criminal Code).\n\n(3) Absolute liability applies to the paragraph (1)(e) element of the\noffence.\nNote:\nFor absolute liability, see section 6.2 of the Criminal Code.\nActing when interested\n\n(4) A person commits an offence if:\n\n(a) the person is a judge or magistrate; and\n\n(b) the judge or magistrate perversely exercises jurisdiction in a\nmatter; and\n\n(c) the judge or magistrate has a personal interest in the matter;\nand"
    },
